# pandas Patterns

## Performance Optimization

- Use PyArrow backend for string columns: `dtype="string[pyarrow]"` for 5-10x faster string ops and 50% memory reduction
- Enable Copy-on-Write: `pd.options.mode.copy_on_write = True` (default in pandas 3.0+)
- Use `.loc[]` or `.iloc[]` for all assignments; chained assignment raises `ChainedAssignmentError`
- Prefer nullable dtypes (`Int64`, `Float64`, `boolean`) over object dtype
- Use method chaining with `.assign()`, `.pipe()`, `.query()`

## Memory Management

- Use `chunksize` parameter in `read_csv()` for large files (>1GB)
- Downcast numeric types: `pd.to_numeric(df['col'], downcast='integer')`
- Use `category` dtype for low-cardinality string columns
- Avoid `inplace=True`; it doesn't save memory with CoW
- Use `del df['col']` or `df.drop(columns=[...])` to free memory

## Best Practices

- Use `pd.read_parquet()` instead of CSV for 10-100x faster I/O
- Set `dtype` explicitly in `read_csv()` to avoid type inference overhead
- Use `groupby()` with `observed=True` for categorical columns
- Prefer `merge()` over `join()` for explicit control
- Use `pd.eval()` for large DataFrame arithmetic operations

## Anti-Patterns

- Never use `df[col][mask] = value` (chained assignment; use `.loc[]`)
- Never load entire large CSV into memory (use chunks or Parquet)
- Never use `apply()` when vectorized operations exist (10-100x slower)
- Never ignore `SettingWithCopyWarning` (fix root cause with `.loc[]`)
- Never use `object` dtype for strings (use `string[pyarrow]`)
